# Jenkins CI/CD

## Jenkins – Build Orchestration Tool

* Jenkins is an open-source build orchestration tool.
* Used to create end-to-end CI/CD pipelines to automate application builds and deployments.
* Can also be used to perform scheduled process automation.

## Jenkins Architecture

Jenkins is based on a **Controller/Master and Agent/Slave** architecture.

### Jenkins Master/Controller

* Install Jenkins.
* Create Jenkins Jobs/Projects/Pipelines.
* Schedule jobs to execute on Jenkins Agent/Slave Nodes.

### Jenkins Slave/Agent Node

* Acts as a Build Server.
* Install required build tools.
* Execute Jenkins Jobs.

### Jenkins Architecture – Scenarios

**Scenario 1: Less Application Build and Release Frequency**

```text
Jenkins_Master (VM)
    |
    +--> Create Jenkins CI/CD Pipeline Projects
    +--> Execute Builds on the same Jenkins Master Node
```

**Scenario 2: Multiple Applications with High Build and Release Frequency**

```text
Jenkins_Master (VM)
    |
    +--> Create Jenkins CI/CD Pipeline Projects
    +--> Schedule Builds on Agent Nodes
             |
             +--> Jenkins_SlaveNode1 - Java Applications
             +--> Jenkins_SlaveNode2 - .NET Applications
             +--> Jenkins_SlaveNode3 - Python Applications
             +--> Jenkins_SlaveNode4 - Angular/NodeJS
             +--> Jenkins_SlaveNode5 - Ruby Applications
```

## Alternatives to Jenkins

* GitHub Actions
* GitLab CI
* Bamboo
* Azure Pipelines
* AWS CodePipeline

## Jenkins CI/CD Workflow

```text
SCM Checkout
     ↓
Application Build
     ↓
Unit Testing
     ↓
Create Artifacts
     ↓
Promote Application to Test Environment
     ↓
Testing / Validation
     ↓
Notify
     ↓
Deploy to Production
```

### Application Build

Application Build is the process of:

* Compiling source code.
* Creating application artifacts/executable binaries.

Examples:

```text
*.war
*.jar
*.exec
*.dll
```

## Developers' Perspective

Developers are consumers of Jenkins tools and services.

They use Jenkins to:

* Execute Jobs/Pipelines.
* Cancel Jobs/Pipelines.
* View Job output.
* View Jenkins workspaces.
* Monitor build and deployment status.

## DevOps Perspective

The DevOps Team owns and manages Jenkins.

### Jenkins Administration Responsibilities

* Installation and configuration of Jenkins.
* Jenkins Global System Configuration.
* Plugin Management.
* Tools Management.
* User Management.
* Security Management.
* Credentials Management.
* Create Jenkins Jobs/Projects.
* Configure Jenkins Controller and Agent Nodes.
* Create CI/CD Pipeline Projects.
* Onboard applications to the DevOps practice.
* Periodic Backup and Recovery.
* Periodic Jenkins and Plugin upgrades.
* Troubleshoot Jenkins-related issues.

